matmul_explicit      jit             1.2          0.5          1.1          0.0

  0 ULP means bit-identical to NumPy at that precision. A fraction of
  a ULP is just the rounding of the float64 reference down to the
  result dtype; >1 ULP on exp/cos means XLA and NumPy picked different
  approximations, which is a result about the libraries rather than an
  error in the harness. The NumPy fp64 column is 0 by construction --
  it is the reference. nan entries are combinations that were not run,
  or that failed validation outright (listed above).

